<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caelian Hill</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caelius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Caelian Hill is one of the seven hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, south-east of the Palatine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tullus-hostilius">Tullus Hostilius</a> settled the people of the destroyed Alba Longa there in the 7th century BCE. Under the Empire it became a district of wealthy houses and later of churches, among them Santi Giovanni e Paolo and Santo Stefano Rotondo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caelian-hill.jpg" alt="Caelian Hill" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roundtheworld · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caelian_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Caelian Hill is a promontory about 2 kilometres long and 400 to 500 metres wide, rising to about 50 metres in the park near the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. According to a tradition recounted by Varro, it took its name from the Etruscan folk hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caelius-vibenna">Caelius Vibenna</a>, who either settled there or was honoured after his death by his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servius-tullius">Servius Tullius</a>. Other authors linked the name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> caelum, &quot;heaven&quot;, and the earlier name was probably Querquetulanus mons, for its oaks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tullus-hostilius">Tullus Hostilius</a> the whole population of Alba Longa was forcibly resettled on the hill. It is said to have been included in the city under Ancus Marcius, and under Augustus it became Regio II Caelimontium. Most of it lay outside the pomerium, so temples to foreign divinities, such as the Temple of Minerva Capta, could be built there. It was a fashionable district of wealthy houses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> records that Mamurra, a soldier who served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, first encrusted the walls of a Roman house with marble there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catullus">Catullus</a> mocked him.</p><p>The Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> was raised on a huge platform, begun by his widow Agrippina after his death and deification in 54 CE and finished under Vespasian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> added a nymphaeum supplied by a branch of the Aqua Claudia. Septimius Severus built a barrack for the mounted imperial guard there between 193 and 197, and the decisive battle of the mint workers' revolt of 271, led by Felicissimus, was fought on the hill. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> was born there in 121. Alaric's sack of 410 badly damaged the buildings, and the hill was increasingly abandoned.</p><p>In the 6th century the hill fell within the ecclesiastical region of the Lateran basilica. Churches followed, including the Basilica of Saints John and Paul, the Basilica of the Four Crowned Martyrs and the Basilica of Saint Stephen in the Round, with monasteries and noble towers, mainly in the 10th and 11th centuries. It suffered again in the sack of 1084.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The hill forms the Rione of the same name and keeps its tradition of care in the Policlinico Militare del Celio. Its monuments include the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, the Arch of Dolabella and the Villa Celimontana. The philosopher George Santayana lived at the Convent of the English Blue Sisters on the hill from 1912 until his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caelian Hill” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Livy, Rome under Tullus Hostilius defeated Alba after the duel of the Horatii and Curiatii in the mid-7th century BCE, razed the city and moved its people to the <b>Caelian Hill</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/alba-longa">Alba Longa</a></li></ul></section>
